  m_Name: 3.QuickStart-WorldSpace
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::Readme
  icon: {fileID: 0}
  title: Quick Start — World Space
  sections:
  - heading: How to Add a Progress Bar
  - : World Space
    text: "1. Create the Material\n\u2022 In your Project window, create a new Material.\n\u2022
      Set the shader to:\\\n  CupOHappiness/Procedural Progress Bar Unlit\n[[IMAGE:0]]
      \n[[IMAGE:1]] \n[[IMAGE:2]] \n[[IMAGE:3]] \n\n\n2. Add a 3D Object\n\u2022
      In your scene, add a 3D Object \u2192 Quad.\n\u2022 Assign the material you
      just created to this quad.\n[[IMAGE:4]] \n[[IMAGE:5]] \n\n3. Set Object Scale\n\u2022
      Make sure the X and Y scale are equal (e.g., Scale = (1, 1, 1)).\n\u2022 In
      the Transform component, enable \u201CConstrain Proportions\u201D to link X,
      Y, and Z.\n\u2022 This ensures the correct aspect ratio.\n\u2022 You can control
      the bar\u2019s vertical height later using the Height setting in the shader.\n[[IMAGE:6]]
      \n\n4. Attach the Script\n\u2022 Add the ProceduralProgressBar script to the
      same GameObject.\n[[IMAGE:7]] \n\n5. Assign the Material in the Script\n\u2022
      In the script component, assign the material:\n\u2022 Either click \u201CGet
      Material\u201D, or drag your custom material manually.\n[[IMAGE:8]] \n\n6.
      You\u2019re now ready to experiment with fill settings, visuals, and shader
      customizations.\n\nTo test:\n\u2022 Adjust the Fill Amount property to see
      the bar animate.\n[[IMAGE:9]]\n\n\u2022 Or click the built-in Testing Buttons
      provided in the script for quick fill previews (only during runtime).\n[[IMAGE:10]] 
      \n\n\n7. Optional: Enable Help Boxes\n\u2022 Keep the \u201CEnable Help Boxes\u201D
      option checked if you\u2019d like in-editor tooltips and guidance.\n\u2022
      Using Example Bars\n[[IMAGE:11]] "
